-- title:
--   Vélu quotient of Tate(qᵖ) by the q-point is Tate(q)
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $p$ be a prime with $p \neq 2$ and with $p$ a unit in $R$. Form the Weierstrass curve [`ModularCurve.tateBase R p`](def/ModularCurve_TateSlots.html#L46) over the Laurent series $R((q))$: it is obtained from [`ModularCurve.tateLaurent R`](def/ModularCurve_TateFormal.html#L86), the curve $\langle 1,0,0,\mathrm{tateA4},\mathrm{tateA6}\rangle$ over $\mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ pushed into $R((q))$ coefficientwise, by applying the ring endomorphism [`ModularCurve.qExpand R p`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25) of $R((q))$ that multiplies the Hahn-series exponents by $p$, i.e. the Tate curve with parameter $q^p$. Let $x$ be the first component of [`ModularCurve.nonToricPoint R p 1 1`](def/ModularCurve_TateSlots.html#L35), the Laurent series attached to the substitution `slotSubst R p 1 1` applied to the bivariate power series [`ModularCurve.tateUnivX`](def/ModularCurve_TateSlots.html#L10). The assertion is the equality of Weierstrass curves over $R((q))$
--   $$\mathrm{quotientByLine}(\mathrm{tateBase}\ R\ p,\ p,\ x) = \mathrm{tateLaurent}\ R,$$
--   where for a curve $W$ and a scalar $x$ the curve $\mathrm{quotientByLine}(W,p,x)$ keeps $a_1,a_2,a_3$ and has $a_4 = W.a_4 - 5t$ and $a_6 = W.a_6 - W.b_2\,t - 7w$, with $t = \sum_{a=1}^{(p-1)/2}\bigl(6\,s_a^2 + W.b_2\,s_a + W.b_4\bigr)$ and $w = \sum_{a=1}^{(p-1)/2}\bigl(W.\Psi_2^{\,2}(s_a) + s_a(6 s_a^2 + W.b_2 s_a + W.b_4)\bigr)$, the $s_a = \mathrm{smulX}\ W\ a\ x$ being the $x$-only multiplication-by-$a$ expressions of $W$ evaluated at $x$. Thus Vélu's $x$-only quotient of the Tate curve with parameter $q^p$ by the line through the non-toric slot point of parameter $q$ is, on the nose, the Tate curve with parameter $q$.
--
--   This is the cusp computation underlying the degeneracy map $(E,P) \mapsto E/\langle P \rangle$ on $X_0$-type moduli: it identifies the Vélu quotient of $\mathrm{Tate}(q^p)$ by the subgroup generated by the point of parameter $q$ with $\mathrm{Tate}(q)$, so that the $c_4$, $c_6$ and discriminant of the quotient are the $q$-expansions of the corresponding Eisenstein and $\Delta$ series. It is used in the construction of Katz modular forms of level $\Gamma_0$ with prescribed value at the cusp, via [`ModularForm.exists_katzGamma0Form_evalCusp_eq_of_five_le`](thm.html#ModularForm.exists_katzGamma0Form_evalCusp_eq_of_five_le).
